%==== 14. Modelling II. =======================================================%

\begin{frame}{Modelling II: Results temporary CSI}
\scriptsize
\begin{block}{Description}
\scriptsize
Temporary CSI is a rare-event classification problem: only 4.74\% of labelled firm-years are positives. Because of this class imbalance, AP and fixed-FPR recall are more decision-relevant than AUC alone. AG Exp. Dataset + VAE ranks best in CV, while AG Expanded Dataset is strongest on the 2016-2019 test split.
\end{block}

\vspace{0.05em}
\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.8pt}
\begin{tabular}{L{2.65cm}R{0.85cm}R{0.85cm}R{0.9cm}R{0.9cm}R{0.9cm}R{0.95cm}}
\toprule
Model & CV-AP & CV-AUC & CV-FPR3 & Test-AP & Test-AUC & Test-FPR3 \\
\midrule
AG Expanded Dataset & 0.2046 & 0.8635 & 0.2344 & \textbf{0.1985} & \textbf{0.8766} & \textbf{0.2002} \\
AG Base Dataset & 0.1923 & 0.8446 & 0.2206 & 0.1656 & 0.8538 & 0.1517 \\
AG Latent Dataset (VAE) & 0.1659 & 0.8454 & 0.1706 & 0.1501 & 0.8438 & 0.1294 \\
AG Exp. Dataset + VAE & \textbf{0.2114} & \textbf{0.8667} & \textbf{0.2478} & 0.1864 & 0.8741 & 0.1779 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\remarkfoot{CV-FPR3 and Test-FPR3 are R@FPR3. Additional model evidence is summarized in Appendix B2--B3.}

\vspace{0.1em}
\begin{alertblock}{Interpretation}
\scriptsize
Test-AP improves from the 4.44\% test prevalence baseline to 19.85\%, showing meaningful concentration of CSI cases near the top of the risk ranking. Test-AUC of 0.8766 confirms strong broad ranking, but AP is the more useful indicator for practical screening. Overall, the results are in-line with the findings by \citet{AlanisChavaShah2022} and \citet{Barboza2017}, who showed that (boosted) tree ensembles reach strong bankruptcy-prediction results.
\end{alertblock}

\end{frame}

%==== 15. Modelling III. ======================================================%

\begin{frame}{Modelling III: Results permanent CSI}
\scriptsize
\begin{block}{Description}
\scriptsize
Permanent CSI is the harder task: positives are rarer than temporary CSI, with only 3.34\% positives in the full labelled sample and 3\% in the test split. Despite this, the models still rank risk well. AG Expanded Dataset remains the reporting baseline, while AG Exp. Dataset + VAE is the strongest non-raw challenger on CV/test.
\end{block}

\vspace{0.05em}
\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.8pt}
\begin{tabular}{L{2.65cm}R{0.85cm}R{0.85cm}R{0.9cm}R{0.9cm}R{0.9cm}R{0.95cm}}
\toprule
Model & CV-AP & CV-AUC & CV-FPR3 & Test-AP & Test-AUC & Test-FPR3 \\
\midrule
AG Expanded Dataset & 0.1854 & \textbf{0.8757} & \textbf{0.2607} & \textbf{0.1416} & 0.8810 & 0.1808 \\
AG Base Dataset & 0.1785 & 0.8677 & 0.2379 & 0.1289 & 0.8627 & 0.1697 \\
AG Latent Dataset (VAE) & 0.1426 & 0.8460 & 0.1960 & 0.1115 & 0.8496 & 0.1513 \\
AG Exp. Dataset + VAE & \textbf{0.1883} & 0.8719 & 0.2578 & 0.1415 & \textbf{0.8838} & \textbf{0.2011} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\remarkfoot{CV-FPR3 and Test-FPR3 are R@FPR3. Additional model evidence is summarized in Appendix B2--B3.}

\vspace{0.1em}
\begin{alertblock}{Interpretation}
\scriptsize
Temporary CSI has more positives and higher test prevalence: 4.44\% vs 3\%. Permanent CSI is therefore harder and more affected by class imbalance. AUC is similar or slightly higher for permanent CSI, but AP is lower because the base prevalence is lower. AG Expanded Dataset and AG Exp. Dataset + VAE are nearly tied on Test-AP: 0.1416 vs 0.1415. AG Exp. Dataset + VAE has the best Test-AUC and Test-FPR3, so it is a strong challenger, but not a clean replacement for the reporting baseline.
\end{alertblock}

\end{frame}


%==== 16. Modelling Summary. ==================================================%

\begin{frame}{Modelling Summary: the models rank implosion risk well}
\footnotesize

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\scriptsize,
  pill/.style={draw=MainColour!70, fill=MainColour!8, rounded corners=4pt,
    text width=2.3cm, align=center, minimum height=0.62cm, inner sep=2pt}]
\node[pill] at (0,0)    {AutoGluon ensemble};
\node[pill] at (3.25,0) {Expanding-window CV};
\node[pill] at (6.5,0) {AP \& recall@FPR};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.3em}
\begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
\begin{column}{0.54\textwidth}
\textbf{Test AP vs naive benchmark} {\scriptsize\color{darkgrey}(prevalence)}

\vspace{0.4em}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\scriptsize, xscale=0.205, yscale=1]
  \draw[darkgrey!55] (0,0) -- (21.5,0);
  \foreach \x in {0,5,10,15,20} {
    \draw[darkgrey!20] (\x,0) -- (\x,3.75);
    \node[anchor=north, darkgrey, scale=0.9] at (\x,-0.04) {\x\%};
  }
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.95] at (0,3.6) {Temporary CSI};
  \fill[darkgrey!60] (0,3.0) rectangle (4.44,3.24);
  \fill[MainColour]  (0,2.64) rectangle (19.85,2.88);
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.85] at (4.7,3.12) {4.4\%};
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.85, text=white] at (0.5,2.76) {19.9\%};
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.95] at (0,2.05) {Permanent CSI};
  \fill[darkgrey!60] (0,1.45) rectangle (3.0,1.69);
  \fill[MainColour]  (0,1.09) rectangle (14.2,1.33);
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.85] at (3.25,1.57) {3.0\%};
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.85, text=white] at (0.5,1.21) {14.2\%};
  \fill[darkgrey!60] (0,0.42) rectangle (0.95,0.62);
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.85] at (1.15,0.52) {Naive Benchmark};
  \fill[MainColour] (0,0.05) rectangle (0.95,0.25);
  \node[anchor=west, scale=0.85] at (1.15,0.15) {Best Test-AP};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.44\textwidth}
\vspace{0.5em}
\textbf{\color{MainColour}Result:} AP $\approx 4.5$--$4.7\times$ the base rate; Test-AUC $\approx 0.88$; recall@FPR3 $\approx 0.20$. AP is the more informative metric under class imbalance for model predictions, even though the AUC score is high \citep{Saito2015}.\\[6pt]
\textbf{\color{MainColour}Interpretation:} \agexp{} is the reporting baseline; \agvae{} the strongest challenger.\\[6pt]
\textbf{\color{MainColour}Subfinding:} VAE augments the base models, but it is not able to replace them.
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\vspace{0.8em}
\begin{block}{Take-Away}
\scriptsize
Calibrated scores become the index exclusion signal. The question expands from \emph{``Can we rank risk?''} to \emph{``Does removing flagged firms using the model predictions improve the risk-return metric of risk-filtered indices?''}
\end{block}
\remarkfoot{Best Test-AP: temporary 0.199, permanent 0.142. Additional metrics are summarized in Appendix B2--B3.}
\end{frame}

%==== 17. False-positive bridge. =============================================%

\begin{frame}{Why the screen cannot be perfect}
\scriptsize
\textbf{The shown example is temporary CSI, \agexp{}.} The exclusion threshold is calibrated on the training set to ensure no data leakage, then carried \emph{unchanged} to the held-out 2016--2019 test set.
\vspace{0.15em}
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\scriptsize, >=stealth,
  b/.style={draw, rounded corners=3pt, align=center, inner sep=4pt, minimum height=0.82cm, text width=4.6cm}]
\node[b, draw=MainColour, fill=MainColour!8] (cv) {\textbf{1.\ Calibrate $\tau$ on CV}\\[-2pt]\tiny $\tau=0.217$ at FPR $=3.0\%$ (recall $23.4\%$)};
\node[b, draw=orange!60!black, fill=orange!12, right=1.0cm of cv] (te) {\textbf{2.\ Apply the same $\tau$ to test}\\[-2pt]\tiny FPR $\to 8.7\%$, recall $\to 45.9\%$};
\draw[->, thick] (cv) -- node[above, font=\tiny]{$\tau$ fixed} (te);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.1em}
\begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
\begin{column}{0.52\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=0.93\linewidth]{slide17_fp_bridge_density.pdf}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.45\textwidth}
\textbf{\color{MainColour}On the test set at this $\tau$}
\begin{itemize}\setlength\itemsep{0.18em}
  \item 369 of 804 CSI firms flagged; 1{,}502 false positives.
  \item Only \textbf{10.8\%} of FPs sit just above $\tau$ (within $[\tau,\tau+0.01]$).
\end{itemize}
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\vspace{-0.05em}
\begin{alertblock}{Conclusions for index construction}
\scriptsize
Many false positives overlap the true-positive scores. A higher threshold (e.g. with FPR5) cannot remove them without dropping real CSI firms.
\end{alertblock}
\remarkfoot{CV: 72{,}223 rows, 3{,}119 positives; test: 18{,}111 rows, 804 positives. \agexp{}, temporary CSI.}
\end{frame}

%==== 18. VAE modelling nuance. =============================================%

\begin{frame}{Modelling IV: The benefits and drawbacks of VAE}
\scriptsize
\textbf{Sub research-question.} How does the integration of Autoencoders for feature engineering impact the Average Precision of Ensemble models compared to those trained solely on raw financial data?

\vspace{0.25em}
\begin{columns}[T,onlytextwidth]
\begin{column}{0.47\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Benefits}
\scriptsize
\begin{itemize}
  \setlength\itemsep{0.22em}
  \item \aglatent{} is considerably smaller in dimension (25 features) compared to \agexp{} (478 features).
  \item \aglatent{} performs reasonably well. AUC is similar, AP is substantially lower on CV and test.
  \item \agvae{} leads CV-AP in both temporary and permanent CSI.
\end{itemize}
\end{block}
\end{column}

\begin{column}{0.47\textwidth}
\begin{block}{Drawbacks}
\scriptsize
\begin{itemize}
  \setlength\itemsep{0.22em}
  \item \aglatent{} cannot fully replicate the informational content of \agexp{}.
  \item \agexp{} retains slightly higher temporary OOS AUC: $0.8961$ compared with $0.8950$ for \agvae{}.
  \item Added architecture and compute cost.
\end{itemize}
\end{block}
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\vspace{0.25em}
\begin{alertblock}{Summary}
\scriptsize
Autoencoder features slightly improve average precision when added to the existing dataset, but do not dominate AUC and AP on their own. Yet, their performance is impressive given the strong reduction in dimensionality.
\end{alertblock}

\end{frame}

%==== 19. Modelling V: Summary and Implications ===============================%

\begin{frame}{Modelling V: Implications for index construction}
\centering
\vspace{0.7em}

\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,
  hero/.style={rounded corners=4pt, minimum height=1.35cm, align=center,
               anchor=north west, inner sep=4pt},
  card/.style={rounded corners=3pt, draw=darkgrey!45, fill=grey!18,
               text width=2.85cm, align=left, minimum height=2.0cm,
               anchor=north west, inner sep=5pt, font=\scriptsize}]

  % ---- labels above the hero ----
  \node[font=\tiny\bfseries, text=darkgrey,   anchor=south west] at (0.05,4.18) {MAIN CONSIDERATION};
  \node[font=\tiny\bfseries, text=MainColour, anchor=south west] at (6.15,4.18) {TAKE-AWAY};

  % ---- hero bar: consideration --(arrow)--> take-away ----
  \node[hero, draw=darkgrey!70, fill=grey!45, text width=3.95cm, font=\scriptsize] (cons) at (0,4.05)
    {The models \textbf{rank risk well}, but false positives are \textbf{frequent and unavoidable}.};
  \node[hero, fill=MainColour, text=white, text width=3.9cm, font=\scriptsize\bfseries] (take) at (6.1,4.05)
    {Difficulties to beat the benchmark's risk-return due to the cost of FPs!};
  \draw[-{Stealth[length=3.5mm,width=5.5mm]}, line width=2.4mm, MainColour]
    (4.45,3.375) -- (5.9,3.375);

  % ---- connector label ----
  \node[font=\scriptsize\itshape, text=darkgrey, anchor=west] at (0.05,2.4) {};

  % ---- three side cards (the side points) ----
  \node[card] (c1) at (0,2.0)
    {\textcolor{MainColour}{\textbf{Low precision}}\\[1pt]
     AUC $\approx 0.88$, but Test-AP $\approx 0.20$ - about \textbf{4 healthy firms} excluded per CSI firm.};
  \node[card] (c2) at (3.6,2.0)
    {\textcolor{warningRed}{\textbf{No clean cutoff}}\\[1pt]
     \textbf{100\%} of false positives overlap true-positive scores; only \textbf{10.8\%} are threshold-adjacent.};
  \node[card] (c3) at (7.2,2.0)
    {\textcolor{MainColour}{\textbf{Threshold drift}}\\[1pt]
     A $3\%$ FPR cutoff on CV becomes \textbf{8.7\%} on test --- out-of-sample drift, not a $3\%$ test-FPR.};

\end{tikzpicture}

\end{frame}

%==== 20 Index Construction E. ===============================================%

\section{Index Construction}
\begin{frame}{Index Construction I: From score to portfolio weight}
\small

\begin{alertblock}{One score rule, two exclusion regimes}
\scriptsize
The model produces a probability of a CSI in the next year. A threshold turns this probability into a binary exclusion flag. The flag is then applied to market-cap weights (similarly to \citet{Campbell2008}). The two regimes differ only in how long the flag persists after it first emerges.
\end{alertblock}

\vspace{0.5em}

\textbf{From model score to portfolio weight}

\vspace{0.3em}
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  font=\scriptsize,
  pipebox/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, minimum height=14mm, text width=3.2cm, align=center, inner sep=3pt},
  scorebox/.style={pipebox, fill=MainColour!12, draw=MainColour},
  flagbox/.style={pipebox, fill=orange!12, draw=orange!60!black},
  weightbox/.style={pipebox, fill=teal!18, draw=teal!60!black},
  pipearrow/.style={->, >=stealth, thick}
]
\node[scorebox] (score) {\textbf{Score}\\[-1pt]\tiny probability of CSI next year\\[-1pt]\tiny};
\node[flagbox, right=8mm of score] (flag) {\textbf{Exclusion flag}\\[-1pt]\tiny 1 if score $\geq$ threshold\\[-1pt]\tiny FPR 1\%, 3\%, 5\%, Youden $J$};
\node[weightbox, right=8mm of flag] (weight) {\textbf{Portfolio weight}\\[-1pt]\tiny market-cap $\times$ $(1-\text{flag})$\\[-1pt]\tiny renormalized};
\draw[pipearrow] (score) -- (flag);
\draw[pipearrow] (flag) -- (weight);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5em}

\textbf{How the flag evolves over time} {\scriptsize\color{darkgrey} --- a firm crosses the threshold in year 3}

\vspace{0.3em}
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[
  font=\scriptsize,
  cell/.style={draw, minimum width=0.85cm, minimum height=0.55cm, inner sep=0pt, anchor=south west},
  held/.style={cell, fill=grey!20, draw=darkgrey},
  flag/.style={cell, fill=warningRed, draw=warningRed!60!black, text=white},
  excl/.style={cell, fill=warningRed!40, draw=warningRed!60!black, text=warningRed!60!black}
]
\node[anchor=east] at (-0.1,1.05) {\scriptsize\textbf{Temporary CSI} \tiny\color{darkgrey}(lockout $L=3$)};
\foreach \i in {0,1} \node[held] at (\i*0.85,0.8) {y\the\numexpr\i+1\relax};
\node[flag] at (1.7,0.8) {y3 $\star$};
\foreach \i in {0,1} \node[excl] at (2.55+\i*0.85,0.8) {y\the\numexpr\i+4\relax};
\foreach \i in {0,1} \node[held] at (4.25+\i*0.85,0.8) {y\the\numexpr\i+6\relax};

\node[anchor=east] at (-0.1,0.20) {\scriptsize\textbf{Permanent CSI} \tiny\color{darkgrey}(absorbing)};
\foreach \i in {0,1} \node[held] at (\i*0.85,-0.05) {y\the\numexpr\i+1\relax};
\node[flag] at (1.7,-0.05) {y3 $\star$};
\foreach \i in {0,...,3} \node[excl] at (2.55+\i*0.85,-0.05) {y\the\numexpr\i+4\relax};

\node[held, anchor=south west] at (0.0,-0.95) {};
\node[anchor=west] at (0.95,-0.78) {\tiny Held};
\node[flag, anchor=south west] at (2.2,-0.95) {};
\node[anchor=west] at (3.15,-0.78) {\tiny First flag ($\star$)};
\node[excl, anchor=south west] at (5.0,-0.95) {};
\node[anchor=west] at (5.95,-0.78) {\tiny Excluded};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\end{frame}

%==== 21. Index Construction I ================================================%

\begin{frame}{Index Construction II: Universes and benchmarks}
\small

\begin{alertblock}{Same exclusion rule, four universes}
\scriptsize
The calibrated score-to-weight methodology is applied within each market-cap universe. Every screened index is judged against its matching market-weighted benchmark.
\end{alertblock}

\vspace{0.55em}
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\scriptsize,
  uni/.style={draw=darkgrey, fill=grey!20, rounded corners=4pt, minimum width=2.7cm, minimum height=1.5cm, align=center}]
\node[uni] at (0,0)   {\textbf{Total Market}\\[1pt]\tiny all eligible firms\\[-1pt]\tiny $\geq$ USD 100 Mio.};
\node[uni] at (3.0,0) {\textbf{Large Cap}\\[1pt]\tiny prior-Dec.\ rank\\[-1pt]\tiny top tier};
\node[uni] at (6.0,0) {\textbf{Mid Cap}\\[1pt]\tiny prior-Dec.\ rank\\[-1pt]\tiny middle tier};
\node[uni] at (9.0,0) {\textbf{Small Cap}\\[1pt]\tiny prior-Dec.\ rank\\[-1pt]\tiny bottom tier};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.5em}
\begin{columns}[t]
\begin{column}{0.49\textwidth}
\textbf{Universe construction}
\scriptsize
\begin{itemize}
  \item Prior-December market cap; minimum USD 100 Mio.
  \item Large/Mid/Small split by market-cap rank.
  \item Quarterly rebalancing.
\end{itemize}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.49\textwidth}
\textbf{Benchmarks}
\scriptsize
\begin{itemize}
  \item Primary: the market-weighted index acts as the main benchmark.
  \item Min-volatility Comparison: will be shown at the end of the presentation.
\end{itemize}
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\vspace{0.3em}
\remarkfoot{The constructed indices match the ones available via the WRDS database. To the misfortune of the author,
WU does not grant access to these prepared index-data.}


\end{frame}

%==== 22. Headline / Verdict (NEW) ===========================================%
\begin{frame}{Index Results: Does the crash filter help?}
\small

\vspace{0.3em}

\begin{columns}[T]
\begin{column}{0.55\textwidth}
\centering
{\scriptsize Alpha relative to the BM by universe (pp, 10 bps net)}\\[3pt]
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\tiny, yscale=0.9]
  \fill[MainColour]  (0,0)   rectangle (0.22,0.22);
  \node[anchor=west] at (0.28,0.11) {OOS 2020--24};
  \fill[posgreenL]   (2.45,0) rectangle (2.67,0.22);
  \node[anchor=west] at (2.73,0.11) {Test 2016--19};
\end{tikzpicture}\\[2pt]
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\tiny, yscale=2.6, xscale=0.78]
  \draw[darkgrey!55] (0,0) -- (7.3,0);
  \foreach \y in {0.2,0.4,0.6,0.8} {
    \draw[darkgrey!22] (0,\y) -- (7.3,\y);
    \node[anchor=east, darkgrey, scale=0.85] at (-0.05,\y) {+\y};
  }
  \foreach \x/\o/\t/\lbl in {0.35/0.42/0.41/Total, 2.15/0.14/0.24/Large,
                             3.95/0.39/0.33/Mid, 5.75/0.55/0.89/Small} {
    \fill[MainColour] (\x,0)      rectangle (\x+0.55,\o);
    \fill[posgreenL]  (\x+0.62,0) rectangle (\x+1.17,\t);
    \node[anchor=north, black] at (\x+0.585,-0.015) {\lbl};
  }
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{column}
\begin{column}{0.43\textwidth}
\scriptsize
\textbf{\color{MainColour}OOS alpha.} Current OOS deltas are positive in all four universes; Mid and Small are strongest.\\[5pt]
\textbf{\color{MainColour}Test-set validation.} The CSI methodology also works within the test set. The Permanent-CSI return/volatility ratio rises from $0.89 \to \mathbf{1.00}$.\\[5pt]
\end{column}
\end{columns}

\begin{alertblock}{Summary}
\scriptsize
The crash-filter appears beneficial in both evaluation windows, but the evidence differs by split. The OOS result is a positive benchmark-relative return delta across the four universes. The test-set results are shown separately and serve as a comparison within a different market regime. Overall, the results are in-line with \citet{Campbell2008} that removing high-risk firms can improve portfolio outcomes.
\end{alertblock}

\end{frame}

%==== 23. Temporary-CSI Results ==========================================%
\begin{frame}{Temporary-CSI results: OOS (2020-2024) with 10 bps of transaction costs}
\scriptsize
\textbf{Best strategy per universe net of 10 bps costs vs.\ the market-weighted BM.}
\vspace{0.3em}

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabular}{L{0.95cm}L{3.1cm}R{1.7cm}R{0.8cm}@{\hspace{1.8em}}R{1.7cm}R{1.9cm}}
\toprule
 & & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Return}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{\color{MainColour}Risk}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4}\cmidrule(lr){5-6}
Universe & Strategy & Geo ret. & $\Delta$ pp & Return/Vol. & Max DD \\
\midrule
Total & \agbase; Youden 3y & $13.3 \to 13.7$ & $+0.42$ & $0.60 \to \mathbf{0.64}$ & $-25.0 \to \mathbf{-23.8}$ \\
Large & \agbase; Youden 3y & $14.3 \to 14.4$ & $+0.14$ & $0.66 \to \mathbf{0.68}$ & $-24.8 \to \mathbf{-23.9}$ \\
Mid   & \agbase; FPR5 5y   & $9.7  \to 10.0$ & $+0.39$ & $0.41 \to \mathbf{0.42}$ & $-24.6 \to \mathbf{-24.5}$ \\
Small & \agvae; Youden 3y  & $7.7  \to 8.2$  & \textcolor{posgreen}{$\mathbf{+0.55}$} & $0.31 \to \mathbf{0.33}$ & $-29.1 \to -29.3$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\vspace{0.1em}
\begin{block}{Main takeaway}
\scriptsize
The risk-filter works better the smaller the market cap.: the alpha rises from $+0.14$pp (Large) to $+0.39$pp (Mid) to $+0.55$pp (Small). Smaller-cap universes include more firms in distress and exclusions move a larger part of the portfolio (about 85-106\% turnover vs.\ 10-13\% for the total market). Drawdown improves everywhere except for small caps.
\end{block}
\end{frame}

%==== 24. Permanent-CSI OOS Results ==========================================%
\begin{frame}{Permanent-CSI results: OOS (2020-2024) with 10 bps of transaction costs}
\scriptsize
\textbf{Best strategy per universe net of 10 bps costs vs.\ the market-weighted BM.}
\vspace{0.3em}

\begin{table}[ht]
\centering
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.25}
\begin{tabular}{L{0.95cm}L{3.1cm}R{1.7cm}R{0.8cm}@{\hspace{1.8em}}R{1.7cm}R{1.9cm}}
\toprule
 & & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{Return}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{\color{MainColour}Risk}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4}\cmidrule(lr){5-6}
Universe & Strategy & Geo ret. & $\Delta$ pp & Return/Vol. & Max DD \\
\midrule
Total & \agvae; FPR5    & $13.3 \to 13.5$ & $+0.26$ & $0.60 \to \mathbf{0.61}$ & $-25.0 \to \mathbf{-24.7}$ \\
Large & \agvae; FPR5    & $14.3 \to 14.5$ & $+0.22$ & $0.66 \to \mathbf{0.67}$ & $-24.8 \to \mathbf{-24.5}$ \\
Mid   & \agbase; FPR5   & $9.7  \to 10.3$ & \textcolor{posgreen}{$\mathbf{+0.62}$} & $0.41 \to \mathbf{0.44}$ & $-24.6 \to \mathbf{-24.5}$ \\
Small & \aglatent; FPR3 & $7.7  \to 7.9$  & $+0.24$ & $0.31 \to \mathbf{0.32}$ & $-29.1 \to -29.3$ \\
