  \vspace{1mm}
  \begin{alertblock}{What it costs to be defensive (geo.\ return delta)}
    \footnotesize
    In the OOS set, the Low-volatility portfolio performs considerably worse than in the test period ($-3.4$\,pp). The CSI model remains stable ($+0.3$\,pp). The maximum drawdowns of the low-volatility portfolio are smaller than those of the benchmark and the CSI model. The results are supported by \citet{Ang2006} and \citet{Moreira2017}, which find that high idiosyncratic volatility stocks earn lower returns and reducing volatility exposure can raise Sharpe ratios. \citet{BlitzVanVliet2007} also finds support for a low-volatility stock anomaly.
  \end{alertblock}
  \remarkfoot{Avg.\ across four universes; 20\,bps costs (Naive Benchmark 0\,bps). CSI permanent tracks dynamic closely.}
\end{frame}

